    {
      "id": "P9Qgo8koAM8xhxuiwcAo-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3452.5960594140283,
      "y": 1911.1913609613146,
      "width": 1164.8485465741003,
      "height": 575,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1989901243,
      "version": 306,
      "versionNonce": 1781180821,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1756792895311,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Very strict about which region the tenant is in and where the Log Analytics workspaces are – they must\nbe in the same region.\n\n- The data lake was enabled because XDR only had one Sentinel (the primary) integrated.\n\n- The data lake does not correctly handle Sentinels that are integrated afterwards.\n\n\n- It is not possible to restore data from the data lake back to the analytic tier for a Sentinel that was\nintegrated afterwards.\n\n- The tenant could only have one data lake, which means that data from different Sentinels ends up mixed\ntogether.\n\n- The same data (e.g., AzureActivity logs) goes into two different Sentinels. Each Sentinel has different\nretention (one keeps 3 months in the analytic tier then 5 years in the data lake, while the other keeps 1\nmonth in the analytic tier then 10 years in the data lake). Since multiple data lakes are not supported,\nhow is retention determined?\n\n- Does the data lake remember which log came from which Sentinel so that RBAC works correctly – meaning\nusers of Sentinel A only see logs that came through Sentinel A, and users of Sentinel B only see the logs\nthat came through Sentinel B? For example, with AzureActivity logs, one group should only see logs from\nsubscription A and the other from subscription B. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Very strict about which region the tenant is in and where the Log Analytics workspaces are – they must be in the same region.\n\n- The data lake was enabled because XDR only had one Sentinel (the primary) integrated.\n\n- The data lake does not correctly handle Sentinels that are integrated afterwards.\n\n\n- It is not possible to restore data from the data lake back to the analytic tier for a Sentinel that was integrated afterwards.\n\n- The tenant could only have one data lake, which means that data from different Sentinels ends up mixed together.\n\n- The same data (e.g., AzureActivity logs) goes into two different Sentinels. Each Sentinel has different retention (one keeps 3 months in the analytic tier then 5 years in the data lake, while the other keeps 1 month in the analytic tier then 10 years in the data lake). Since multiple data lakes are not supported, how is retention determined?\n\n- Does the data lake remember which log came from which Sentinel so that RBAC works correctly – meaning users of Sentinel A only see logs that came through Sentinel A, and users of Sentinel B only see the logs that came through Sentinel B? For example, with AzureActivity logs, one group should only see logs from subscription A and the other from subscription B. ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
